  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B039",
        "laureates": [
          "Michael Berry",
          "Yakir Aharonov"
        ],
        "citation": "for revealing the observable geometric and electromagnetic structure of quantum phase"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B001",
    "B058"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "B039 identifies a deep, experimentally established change in the physical meaning of phase. The Aharonov-Bohm effect and Berry phase show, in complementary settings, that phase records physically observable structure beyond local classical forces or energies. Their consequences extend from quantum foundations to condensed matter and precision physics. Berry and Aharonov are each directly and unmistakably associated with the central phenomena, and the long interval since their introduction demonstrates both maturity and continuing generative power.",
    "case_against": "The principal reservation is that the Aharonov-Bohm effect and Berry phase have distinct historical origins and mechanisms. A unified citation must therefore be narrow and conceptually honest, rather than implying that one was merely a variant or consequence of the other; historical review should also establish whether further indispensable contributors are excluded.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson argues that combining the Aharonov-Bohm effect and Berry phase may obscure their distinct histories and additional essential contributors.",
        "response": "I agree that this is the central attribution risk and would not support a loose label such as a prize for all topological phases. But the shared lesson is sufficiently precise: quantum phase can encode observable electromagnetic or geometric structure. The proposed two-person, two-phenomenon framing preserves the distinct routes while recognizing their common conceptual transformation."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson emphasizes that aberration correction in electron microscopy has produced unambiguous, durable, atomic-resolution impact, while noting a substantial technical lineage.",
        "response": "B001 is an exceptionally strong alternative and its union of theoretical and instrumental achievement is attractive. Yet its very broad lineage makes the three-name allocation less settled than B039's attribution. For this round I give greater weight to the unusually clean foundational insight of B039, while retaining B001 as the leading experimental alternative."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson supports B039 but stresses that its conceptual scope needs a precise prize framing.",
        "response": "I concur. The citation should avoid treating the effects as identical and should focus specifically on the observable structure of quantum phase, which captures their common physical significance without erasing their different realizations."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether archival historical assessment supports treating the two phase phenomena as one sufficiently unified achievement.",
      "Whether the proposed citation adequately distinguishes the two discoveries while remaining coherent."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
